      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>An Ofsted early years inspection is easier to face when you have already run it on yourself. Early years provision in England is regulated by Ofsted, the counterpart to Scotland's Care Inspectorate, and not by CQC, which regulates health and adult social care. A mock inspection is simply self-evaluation done deliberately and in inspection order: you walk the setting against the Early Years Foundation Stage the way an inspector would, judge honestly, and capture the evidence at the point you observe it. The value is not the score you give yourself. It is the list of gaps you find while there is still time to close them, and the improvement plan you leave behind.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>A mock inspection walks the same ground a real Ofsted early years inspection would: quality of education, behaviour and attitudes, personal development, leadership and management, and the safeguarding and welfare requirements.</li><li>Early years provision in England is regulated by Ofsted, not the Care Inspectorate, which covers Scotland, and not CQC. The statutory framework is the Early Years Foundation Stage.</li><li>Ratios and safeguarding are the non-negotiables: ratios kept, suitable people recruited safely, premises and first aid in line with the EYFS.</li><li>The mock is worth nothing unless it drives action. Each finding becomes an action with an owner and a date; running it does not raise the grade, acting on it does.</li><li>Quickler runs the mock as a WhatsApp walk and captures the evidence. It is not a children's records, learning journal, register or child protection system, and holds no child's personal data.</li><li>Quickler is not affiliated with or endorsed by Ofsted, and whether your evidence satisfies an inspection is a judgement for you and your inspector.</li></ul>"
