      "heading": "What an Ofsted early years inspection looks at",
      "html": "<p>An Ofsted early years inspection looks at provision under the Early Years Foundation Stage and Ofsted's education inspection approach. In general terms it considers the quality of education, meaning the curriculum across the EYFS areas of learning and how children build knowledge and make progress; behaviour and attitudes and personal development, meaning how settled, engaged and independent children are and how the setting supports their wider development; leadership and management, meaning how clearly and accountably the setting is run and how honestly it evaluates itself; and, sitting under all of it, the safeguarding and welfare requirements: ratios, suitable people and safer recruitment, premises and safety, paediatric first aid, health and the key person approach.</p><p>Ofsted reports a graded judgement and is reforming how it grades and reports, so describe grading in general terms and work from the current published Ofsted early years inspection handbook rather than quoting exact grade labels, which are changing. Treat this page as general information, not regulatory advice.</p>"
