      "eyebrow": "How to run it",
      "heading": "Running the mock on yourself",
      "html": "<p>A mock inspection is self-evaluation done deliberately, in inspection order, by someone willing to be honest. Pick a person to run it, the manager, a deputy, or a peer from another setting, and have them walk the provision the way an inspector would rather than sitting with a form. Start with the safeguarding and welfare requirements, because those are the non-negotiables: watch the ratio at the busiest part of the day, check the safer-recruitment file and DBS checks, confirm paediatric first aid cover, walk the premises and the outdoor space, and test whether the safeguarding lead and the team actually know their duties.</p><p>Then take the curriculum in practice: watch teaching and play, see what children can do, and judge whether the curriculum is planned and children progress through it. Capture each finding at the point you observe it, honestly, including the uncomfortable ones. The output that matters is not a score. It is the list of gaps and an improvement plan with owners and dates, which you can re-run before the real inspection to see what closed.</p>"
